  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 207 PM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nThe seasonably deep upper low over the Southwest CONUS continues to \ngradually fill this afternoon, still pumping deep moisture \nnorthwards across the Rockies and High Plains. Upper ridging \ncentered over the Mississippi Valley is preventing this low from \nmoving much east. There is a weak cold front approaching northern \nKS, but it is progged to wash out this evening and not be much of a \nfactor going forward. As a result, warm and dry conditions remain in \nplace across the forecast area, and will continue through Sunday.\n\nBy Sunday night into Tuesday, a second upper low will move into the \nPacific Northwest, lifting the original low north-northeast across \nthe Plains. The best moisture transport and lift looks to remain \nwest of the area, so appreciable rainfall is not expected. However a \nfew rounds of light rain still seem plausible. This would initially \nbe towards central KS on Monday, then farther east on Tuesday as a \nsecondary vorticity maxima lifts northeast. Clouds may keep \ntemperatures Tuesday down a bit, but overall warm temperatures will \ncontinue without any notable cold front.\n\nThe warm pattern will hold for the rest of the week too, as \ntroughing amplifies out west with broad ridging expanding over the \neastern CONUS. A few more weak perturbations will move through the \nsouthwesterly flow aloft, which will keep some low PoPs around each \nday. However the better chances for any widespread rain will hold \noff until the main western trough moves east towards the Plains. \nThis looks to be around or after the end of the current forecast \nperiod, likely not until next weekend.",
